  "sport": "hockey",
  "title": "British Bulldogs – get across without losing the puck",
  "description": "All players stand on one side with a puck each. One chaser – the bulldog – stands in the middle. On the signal everyone skates with the puck across to the other side, and the bulldog tries to win as many pucks as possible. Whoever loses their puck becomes a bulldog too. The more who are caught, the harder it gets to cross.\n\nLevels: 1) The bulldogs have sticks and take the puck. 2) Every attacker also has a tail (a bib tucked into the waistband) that works as an extra life – lose the puck but keep the tail and you get a new puck. 3) The bulldogs have no sticks and only need to take the tail. Fit the area to the number of players. The diagram is not to scale.",
  "coachingPoints": [
    {
      "id": "cp1",
      "text": "Look up – find the gap between the bulldogs."
    },
    {
      "id": "cp2",
      "text": "Change direction and speed when a bulldog comes close."
    },
    {
      "id": "cp3",
      "text": "Shield the puck with your body if you are put under pressure."
    }
  ],
  "locale": "en",
